If absolutely **nothing** is happening (not even the "Downloading Excel..." blue notification popping up), it usually means one of three things:
 1. The function isn't connected to the button properly.
 2. The viewingBatchId is somehow reading as empty, causing it to silently stop at the if (!viewingBatchId) return; line.
 3. The React app or Spring Boot server needs a quick restart to register the new code.
Let's replace your handleDownloadExcel function with a highly detailed "debug" version. This will pop up notifications at every step so we can see exactly where it is stopping.
### 1. Replace the Function in JournalAuthorization.js
Replace your current handleDownloadExcel function (inside the component) with this one:
```javascript
  const handleDownloadExcel = async () => {
    // 1. Check if button click registers
    console.log("Export button clicked! Batch ID is:", viewingBatchId);

    if (!viewingBatchId) {
      showSnackBar("Error: Batch ID is missing!", "error");
      return;
    }
    
    try {
      // 2. Show starting notification
      showSnackBar("Downloading Excel...", "info");
      console.log("Calling API endpoint...");
      
      // 3. Call the API
      const response = await callApi(
        `/JS/journals/download-batch/${viewingBatchId}`, 
        null, 
        "GET", 
        "blob", 
        null, 
        {}, 
        false
      );
      
      console.log("API Response received:", response);

      const blobData = response.data || response;
      if (!blobData) {
        throw new Error("No data received from backend");
      }

      // 4. Create and download the file
      const blob = new Blob([blobData], { type: "application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.spreadsheetml.sheet" });
      const url = window.URL.createObjectURL(blob);
      
      const link = document.createElement("a");
      link.href = url;
      link.setAttribute("download", `Batch_${viewingBatchId}.xlsx`);
      document.body.appendChild(link);
      link.click();
      link.remove();
      window.URL.revokeObjectURL(url);
      
      showSnackBar("Download complete.", "success");
    } catch (e) {
      console.error("Download failed:", e);
      let errorMsg = "Failed to download Excel file.";
      if (e.response && e.status === 404) {
          errorMsg = "API endpoint not found (404).";
      }
      showSnackBar(errorMsg, "error");
    }
  };

```
### 2. Things to Check After Saving:
 1. **Did you restart Spring Boot?** Because we added a brand new @GetMapping("/download-batch/{batchId}") to your Java Controller, you **must** stop and restart your Spring Boot application, or the frontend won't be able to find the URL.
 2. **Press F12 in your browser:** Open your browser's Developer Tools, go to the **Console** tab, and click the Export Excel button. Look at the text that prints out.
   * If it prints "Export button clicked!" but fails at the API, your backend path might be slightly different (e.g., maybe it needs to be /JS/download-batch/... without /journals/ depending on your Controller mapping).
